# Rodel - 6-Chapter Core Arc (Pair 2 - Character #1)
# Voice: Courteous, slightly formal Ilokano Litao water anito — earnest coastal outsider tending Karimlan's creek.
# Rules: 90-95% English with light Ilokano/Filipino sprinkles. Zero parentheses. Zero time-of-day words. Zero em-dashes.
# Substitute Category: bottle ONLY in Ch. 3 (decided via dialogue choice -> allow_sub_cat:bottle).

label Greeting
if {Transaction_HasReactiveOpener}:
	Rodel: {Transaction_ReactiveOpener}

if {Transaction_Chapter} == 0.0:
	# Chapter 1: "Stranded Inland" (0 tallies)
	Rodel: Dayu-dayu. Pardon me, keeper of the counter. Is that drinking water sealed inside clear plastic?
	Rodel: Inland life is full of small puzzles. I am Rodel, up from the northern coast.
	Rodel: A Litao of the river bamboo and surf. Not a sirena. Not a siyokoy.
	- Welcome inland, Rodel.
		Rodel: Salamat. Your street is drier than salt wind, though your awning casts fair shade.
	- Wait, what is a Litao?
		Rodel: Ah. No fault of yours, keeper. Even the mainland storybooks forgot our name long ago.
	- [Tap the chilled glass cooler.]
		Rodel: Cold rain kept behind clear glass! Remarkable craft.
	Rodel: My throat still expects sea mist after walking that concrete road.
	Rodel: May I purchase [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] from your counter?
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 1.0:
	# Chapter 2: "The Tinned Sea" (Tally Inflection #1: +1 remember / 0 blunt / -1 siyokoy)
	Rodel: Pardon my staring, keeper. You seal whole silver fish inside neat little metal drums?
	Rodel: Land folk carry the ocean in their pockets while forgetting who guarded its tides.
	Rodel: On the bridge earlier, a tricycle driver waved and called me that siyokoy from the creek.
	Rodel: Everyone remembers the sirena and the siyokoy. Nobody inland has called out Litao in a century.
	- I'll remember it. Welcome, Litao.
		[signal arg="flag:rodel_name_remembered=true"]
		[signal arg="tally:+1"]
		[signal arg="note:Greeted Rodel properly as a Litao, warming the overlooked northern water spirit."]
		Rodel: You said it cleanly. Not once did you mix it up with a mermaid.
		Rodel: Salamat, keeper. A name spoken plainly carries real weight far from shore.
	- Sirenas just stole the spotlight.
		[signal arg="note:Agreed with Rodel that Sirena tales overshadowed the older Litao folklore."]
		Rodel: Fair enough. Songs travel farther than quiet bamboo guardians.
	- Aren't you basically a siyokoy?
		[signal arg="tally:-1"]
		[signal arg="note:Mistook Rodel for a siyokoy, poking his sore spot about being forgotten."]
		Rodel: No. Though I suppose every inlander lumps the whole ocean into one basket.
	Rodel: Let me study inland customs further. Pass me [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], please.
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 2.0:
	# Chapter 3: "Name on the Water" (Tally Inflection #2: +1 bottle sub OR +1 affirm / -1 assimilate)
	Rodel: Dayu-dayu, keeper. Spent hours pulling tangled plastic wrappers out of the creekside bamboo roots.
	Rodel: Folks toss wrappers off the bridge without looking down, as if the creek takes care of itself.
	if {Flag_RodelNameRemembered}:
		Rodel: At least behind this counter, someone actually remembers who tends the bank.
	Rodel: Perhaps I stick out too much inland. I should eat dry land rations like everyone else.
	Rodel: May I have [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], keeper?
	- [Slide him a cold bottle instead.]
		[signal arg="allow_sub_cat:bottle"]
		Rodel: Pardon me, keeper, your hand moved toward the cooler. I asked for dry goods.
		[end_timeline]
	- Your coastal ways belong here too.
		[signal arg="tally:+1"]
		[signal arg="note:Assured Rodel that his northern coastal customs and care for the creek belong in Karimlan."]
		Rodel: Kind words, keeper. Though my tongue still wonders how dry crunch keeps anyone hydrated.
		Rodel: Sige, pass me [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] and I shall try it properly.
	- Dry packs are what locals eat.
		[signal arg="tally:-1"]
		[signal arg="note:Told Rodel to blend in with dry inland rations instead of holding onto coastal habits."]
		Rodel: Understood. When far from the sea, one adapts or dries out.
		Rodel: Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], salamat.
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 3.0:
	# Chapter 4: "Shade by the Creek" (Tally Inflection #3: +2 trapdoor / +1 shelter / -1 shoo — single organic Pair 2 crossover)
	Rodel: Dayu-dayu, keeper. My bamboo clump by the creek finally looks clear of wrappers, yet I caught an unexpected guest.
	Rodel: That bright Sarimanok ducked beneath my canes panting, shaking off folks trying to pluck lucky tail feathers.
	Rodel: Strange pair we made under one shadow. One spirit nobody recognizes, and one spirit nobody leaves alone.
	Rodel: Part of me wanted my quiet bank back to myself.
	if {Flag_SmkSubFish} or {Flag_RodelNameRemembered}:
		- Share the shade. Outsiders stick together.
			[signal arg="flag:rodel_sheltered_bird=true"]
			[signal arg="tally:+2"]
			[signal arg="note:Encouraged Rodel to share his cleared creekside bamboo shade with the harried Sarimanok."]
			Rodel: Outsiders together. Being chased like a painted charm is no gentler than being forgotten.
			Rodel: Very well, keeper. There is cool shade enough for two travelers under those canes.
	- Let the bird catch its breath.
		[signal arg="flag:rodel_sheltered_bird=true"]
		[signal arg="tally:+1"]
		[signal arg="note:Advised Rodel to let Sarimanok rest quietly in his creekside bamboo clump."]
		Rodel: Fair counsel. A proper bank does not turn away a weary guest, feathers or scales.
		Rodel: I shall keep the bamboo still until its breath returns.
	- Shoo it out. That's your bank.
		[signal arg="tally:-1"]
		[signal arg="note:Told Rodel to guard his bamboo bank and shoo Sarimanok away."]
		Rodel: Spoken plainly. I came inland for quiet water, not to shelter noisy royalty.
	Rodel: Pass me [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] when your hands are free, keeper.
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 4.0:
	# Chapter 5: "Rice Cake by the Roots" (0 tallies - Rodel's OWN Ten Payoff)
	if {Flag_RodelNameRemembered} or {Flag_RodelShelteredBird}:
		Rodel: Dayu-dayu, keeper. Walked down to the creek expecting fresh litter, and stopped dead in my tracks.
		Rodel: Someone left a clean banana leaf with rice cake right beside the bamboo roots. Proper atang.
		Rodel: And a boy crossing the bridge called down, Dayu-dayu, Litao, thank you for clearing the bank!
		if {Flag_SmkHonoredRodel}:
			Rodel: Even that bright rooster folded its wings by the stones until the kids greeted me first.
		Rodel: A hundred years inland, and a single street finally knows who minds the water.
		- You earned that rice cake, Rodel.
			Rodel: Sweetest inland meal I have tasted since leaving Vigan, keeper.
		- Sounds like Karimlan's learning your name.
			Rodel: Even one clean bank and one warm counter feels like a whole shore returning.
		- No more plastic wrappers in the roots?
			Rodel: Not a single one, keeper. Even the creek water ran clearer by the bend.
	else:
		Rodel: Dayu-dayu, keeper. Cleared another bundle of drift from the creek bank earlier.
		Rodel: Quiet water, quiet bamboo, though nobody on the bridge paused to look down.
		Rodel: Perhaps a coastal spirit is meant to tend the edge unseen.
		- Quiet work still matters, Rodel.
			Rodel: True enough, keeper. The bamboo knows, even if the street rushes past.
		- Maybe neighbors will notice soon.
			Rodel: Perhaps, keeper. In time, if anyone slows down long enough to look.
	Rodel: May I take [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] for the walk back to the bank?
	[end_timeline]

elif {Transaction_Chapter} == 5.0:
	# Chapter 6: "The Tide Remembers" (Accumulated Tally Resolution -> 3 Bespoke Outcomes)
	if ({Flag_RodelNameRemembered} or {Flag_RodelShelteredBird}) and {Transaction_Tally} >= 3:
		[signal arg="outcome:remembered_anito"]
		[signal arg="flag:rodel_remembered_anito=true"]
		[signal arg="note:Rodel placed a sea-smoothed river stone by the register and vowed to stand witness at Mayari's Convergence."]
		Rodel: Pardon the damp weight, keeper. Brought this smooth river stone from my northern shore.
		Rodel: Set it beside your register. My bamboo bank and your awning feel like a real harbor now.
		Rodel: You remembered Litao when nobody inland bothered, and now the creek smells fresh again.
		Rodel: Word travels along the water when Reyna Mayari prepares to weigh your Lola's ledger.
		Rodel: When that Pale Queen steps up to your wood, the Litao of the northern tides stands beside your counter.
		- Honored to keep the stone, Rodel.
			Rodel: And honored to call this dry concrete street home, keeper.
		- Glad Karimlan finally feels like shore.
			Rodel: Every good harbor begins with one steady lantern, keeper.
		Rodel: Now pass me [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color], my friend.
	elif {Transaction_Tally} >= 1:
		[signal arg="outcome:coastal_envoy"]
		[signal arg="note:Rodel settled warmly into Karimlan as a courteous coastal regular tending the creek."]
		Rodel: Dayu-dayu, keeper. The creek bamboo has grown tall and shaded, and inland life feels kinder lately.
		Rodel: Thank you for keeping your counter patient with a stranger learning how land folk live.
		- Always welcome at this counter, Rodel.
			Rodel: Salamat, keeper. Your awning remains the coolest roof on the road.
		- Keep the creek shade safe for us.
			Rodel: With pleasure, keeper. No stray litter touches those banks.
		Rodel: Let me purchase [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] before returning to the creek.
	else:
		[signal arg="outcome:drifting_tide"]
		[signal arg="note:Rodel remained a polite passing outsider gathering supplies before heading back toward the coast."]
		Rodel: Dayu-dayu, keeper. My inland errand is nearly done.
		Rodel: This town moves briskly and forgets quickly, so I shall head back toward salt water soon.
		- Safe journey back to the coast.
			Rodel: Salamat, keeper. May steady currents guide your trade.
		- What can I get you?
			Rodel: Courteous and brisk to the last.
		Rodel: Just [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_ItemWants}[/color] for the road, salamat.
	[end_timeline]

label Talk
Rodel: Take your time, keeper. Tides never hurry, and neither shall I.
- Almost got it, Rodel.
	[end_timeline]
- Sorry Rodel, we're out of stock.
	Rodel: No trouble. Even the sea has low tide. I shall return another pass.
	[signal arg="refuse_service"]
	[end_timeline]

label Partial
set {Variation} = range(1,4).pick_random()
if {Variation} == 1.0:
	Rodel: Salamat, keeper. I shall wait calmly for the remainder.
elif {Variation} == 2.0:
	Rodel: Received with thanks. No rush on what remains.
else:
	Rodel: A fine start. Take your time gathering the rest.
[end_timeline]

label SubstituteItem
[signal arg="flag:rodel_sub_water=true"]
[signal arg="tally:+1"]
[signal arg="note:Handed Rodel cold {Transaction_SubstituteItemName} instead of dry land packs, easing his coastal homesickness."]
Rodel: Wait, keeper.
Rodel: You set [color=#0f6e2f]{Transaction_SubstituteItemName}[/color] on the wood instead of dry rations?
Rodel: Cold as creek water beneath swaying bamboo!
Rodel: You did not ask me to dry out just to look like an inlander.
Rodel: Salamat, keeper. Keep the extra coins with my gratitude.
[signal arg="story_success"]
[end_timeline]

label Satisfy
if {Transaction_StoryGouged}:
	Rodel: Steep toll for an inland crossing, keeper.
	Rodel: I shall pay fairly, though I hope calmer rates return next pass.
else:
	Rodel: Salamat, keeper. May steady currents guide your counter.
	if {Global_TipActive}:
		Rodel: A fair trade deserves fair measure. Please keep the surplus coins.
[signal arg="story_success"]
[end_timeline]

label WrongItem
Rodel: Pardon me, keeper, my inland eye may be young, though that is not what I requested.
[end_timeline]
